{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6efe19cd331a326","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9005-E003\n3\ttitle: Determine required preemption semantics and checkpoint overhead\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9005\n5\tcomponent: Distributed Workloads\n6\tteam: Distributed Workloads\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 0\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 1\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Determine required preemption semantics and checkpoint overhead\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis investigation resolves the strategy's third open question: what\n30\tpreemption semantics reference customers actually need — full eviction with\n31\trequeue, or checkpoint-aware graceful preemption. Field signals are\n32\tconflicting and no benchmark of checkpoint overhead exists. The preemption\n33\timplementation epics are gated on this outcome.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Consolidate the conflicting field signals and enumerate the concrete\n38\t  preemption scenarios reference customers run (job sizes, frequency of\n39\t  preemption, tolerance for lost work).\n40\t- Benchmark training-job checkpoint overhead (checkpoint size, write time,\n41\t  restore time) for representative multi-node training workloads, locally\n42\t  and, where needed, on GPU hardware.\n43\t- Determine whether checkpoint-restore cost makes graceful preemption\n44\t  worthwhile versus full eviction with requeue.\n45\t- Produce a decision document with one of two bounded outcomes:\n46\t  - **Outcome A**: full eviction with requeue satisfies customer needs.\n47\t  - **Outcome B**: checkpoint-aware graceful preemption is required.\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- Given representative training workloads, when checkpoint and restore\n52\t  overhead is measured, then a benchmark report quantifies the cost of\n53\t  graceful preemption versus re-running from scratch.\n54\t- Given the field signals and benchmark, when preemption semantics are\n55\t  decided, then a decision document records the chosen semantics and the\n56\t  customer evidence behind them, so the gated preemption implementation can\n57\t  start.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- HLR-4 (P1): higher-priority workloads preempt lower-priority ones per\n62\t  admin-defined policy — this investigation determines which preemption\n63\t  semantics the implementation must deliver.\n64\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-951e480b12557321","agent_id":"ab362f693a2ccaac7","session_id":"175a4578-f41d-49eb-8efd-6ff76cbc31ff","uuid":"3ed33480-4d97-493a-bf29-54a24fbeb931","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:44:09.347Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6efe19cd331a326","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9005 decomposition"}
