{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95c70675f44290b9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E010\n3\ttitle: PyTorchJob checkpoint support for graceful preemption\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9005\n5\tcomponent: Training Operator\n6\tteam: Distributed Workloads\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9005-E003\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-9005-E003\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: remove\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the preemption investigation finds full eviction with requeue\n16\t    sufficient, the checkpoint capability is not needed and this work is dropped.\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 0\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: 0\n23\t  external_dependency: 0\n24\t  human_process_gates: 0\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 0\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: High\n30\tai_implementability_score: 4\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\t---\n33\t# PyTorchJob checkpoint support for graceful preemption\n34\t\n35\t## Description\n36\t\n37\tBranch B of the preemption implementation, Training Operator side (applies\n38\twhen the preemption semantics investigation concludes checkpoint-aware\n39\tgraceful preemption is required): provide checkpoint support for multi-node\n40\tPyTorchJobs so a job's progress can be captured before preemption and\n41\trestored after requeue, following the upstream Training Operator checkpoint\n42\tmechanism where applicable.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\t- Enable/extend PyTorchJob checkpointing in the midstream Training Operator\n47\t  so multi-node jobs can write checkpoints on demand (before preemption).\n48\t- Support restoring a job from its latest checkpoint when it is re-admitted\n49\t  after preemption.\n50\t- Validate checkpoint and restore correctness for representative multi-node\n51\t  training jobs, with overhead consistent with the preemption benchmark.\n52\t\n53\t## Acceptance Criteria\n54\t\n55\t- Given a running multi-node PyTorchJob, when a checkpoint is requested, then\n56\t  job progress is checkpointed and the job continues or stops cleanly.\n57\t- Given a PyTorchJob re-admitted after preemption, when it restarts, then it\n58\t  restores from the latest checkpoint and training resumes.\n59\t- Given representative jobs, when checkpoint/restore is exercised, then\n60\t  overhead stays within the bounds established by the preemption semantics\n61\t  benchmark.\n62\t\n63\t## HLR Traceability\n64\t\n65\t- HLR-4 (P1): higher-priority workloads preempt lower-priority ones per\n66\t  admin-defined policy — this epic provides the checkpoint capability that\n67\t  graceful preemption builds on.\n68\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e132ce62e656773","agent_id":"a68d4b19d59b431d6","session_id":"175a4578-f41d-49eb-8efd-6ff76cbc31ff","uuid":"842fc84b-ff3b-4630-b70e-aa4d198acb86","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:56:18.863Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-95c70675f44290b9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9005"}
